{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 7, :comments ["<b>Everyone [whom I have called by My name, etc.]</b> Some say [the word] נקרא is like קורא (one who calls), but this is not correct; rather it is as it sounds — everyone who is called by My name, who are the people of YHWH:" "<b>And for My glory.</b> That there be glory for My name:" "<b>I created him.</b> This people:" "<b>Even I made him.</b> The sense is 'and I established him.' And Rabbi Solomon the author of the Songs of Equivalence, of blessed memory, interpreted it as referring to the light of the world, but it does not adhere to the sense of the pericope:"], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 43, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra rejects reading the niphal נקרא as an active participle; 'as it sounds' means the passive sense, 'is called.'", 3 "The Hebrew reads השקולי' (possibly השקוליו\"ת, 'of the Equilibria/Equivalences'); the work's precise title is uncertain and rendered here as 'Songs of Equivalence.'"}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:57:46.292353822Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}